course: dictionary
lesson: 7068
title: Patina: the look that comes with age
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe a colour or shine that only time can make. Patina is one of them. Read this: «The Statue of Liberty is made of copper sheets; it was shiny brown in 1886 and turned green as a protective coat called patina formed.» The clues are shiny brown, turned green and protective coat. So patina must be a layer that forms over time and protects the metal.
explain: Now read this: «Silver turns black from sulphur gases, which eggs give off; gold hardly ever tarnishes, and copper slowly grows a green coat called patina.» The word slowly is a clue. Patina grows over a long time, not in a day.
explain: Patina can also mean the soft shine or worn look that old wood, leather or stone gets from age and use. When you meet it, ask yourself: what would this look like? Then check that your meaning still makes sense in the sentence.
fact: A patina is a thin layer that forms slowly on some metals, such as copper and bronze.
fact: The Statue of Liberty was shiny brown copper in 1886, and it turned green as its patina formed.
fact: A green patina on copper shows a chemical reaction between the metal, air and water.
fact: Patina can also mean the soft shine that old wood, leather or stone gets from age and use.
fact: In art and antiques, patina makes old things more valued.
exercise: «The Statue of Liberty was shiny brown in 1886 and turned green.» What is the green coat called? | *Patina | Sulphur | Copper sheets | Gold
exercise: «Copper slowly grows a green coat called patina.» What does slowly tell you about patina? | It forms very fast, in a few minutes | It never forms at all | *It forms over a long time | It forms only in winter
exercise: «Silver turns black from sulphur gases, which eggs give off.» Which word in this sentence names a gas that makes silver black? | *sulphur | turns | eggs | silver
test: «The Statue of Liberty is made of copper sheets.» What is the statue made of? | *Copper | Wood | Stone | Gold
test: In art and antiques, what does patina do to old things? | *It makes them more valued | It makes them brand new | It hides them from view | It makes them weigh less
exam: Which meaning fits best in «a patina of calm»? | *A thin outer look that seems calm | A loud, noisy crowd | A cold, wet winter day | A deep hole in the ground
